  'Modern and Contemporary Art',
  'Students begin with why modern art emerged from industrialization, colonial modernity, urban life, photography, and new models of perception, then follow the shift from illusionistic representation toward fragmented form, abstraction, and artistic self-consciousness, including Dada, Surrealism, design, and modern media. Later work covers postwar abstraction, Pop, mass culture, Minimalism, Conceptual art, expanded media, feminist, queer, Black, Indigenous, and decolonial histories, identity and the body, global circuits, institutions and markets, public and activist practice, and twenty-first-century digital, ecological, and networked art, with art writing and a comparative research capstone.',
  $mocajson${"slug": "modern-and-contemporary-art", "title": "Modern and Contemporary Art", "description": "Students begin with why modern art emerged from industrialization, colonial modernity, urban life, photography, and new models of perception, then follow the shift from illusionistic representation toward fragmented form, abstraction, and artistic self-consciousness, including Dada, Surrealism, design, and modern media. Later work covers postwar abstraction, Pop, mass culture, Minimalism, Conceptual art, expanded media, feminist, queer, Black, Indigenous, and decolonial histories, identity and the body, global circuits, institutions and markets, public and activist practice, and twenty-first-century digital, ecological, and networked art, with art writing and a comparative research capstone.", "topics": [{"id": "4eefcc1b-fd75-4b7d-85a3-76234366604a", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins of Modernity and the Late Nineteenth-Century Break", "description": "Students begin by understanding why modern art emerged: industrialization, colonial modernity, urban life, photography, and new models of perception reshaped what art could do.", "children": [{"id": "377b30fc-b151-4583-86a3-5f36e15dea72", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernity, urban life, and new publics", "children": [{"id": "8b547ea3-cd4e-4432-a1e4-9137ab6f78c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrialization, capitalism, and the modern city"}, {"id": "b66de36e-bb95-4236-9dc1-5af3b1d9cf73", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions, salons, academies, dealers, and critics"}, {"id": "289a85ee-3290-4e74-a2fe-ca19f27dee27", "type": "concept", "title": "Changing class, gender, labor, and leisure cultures"}], "description": "Students learn that modern art was not just a style but a response to rapid social change, new cities, new audiences, and new institutions."}, {"id": "3d2ce1bd-2d0f-4a13-a7b3-e9af8fa637b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Photography, perception, and the crisis of representation", "children": [{"id": "5f12b74f-e633-472f-bfce-f4f7eedbc168", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and mechanical reproduction"}, {"id": "64406474-d821-4291-b41d-629d36146490", "type": "concept", "title": "Optics, speed, motion, and modern vision"}, {"id": "1c77abe2-ce4b-4016-8838-07ce4f0fd2f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Sketch, fragment, surface, and subjective perception"}], "description": "This subtopic explains why artists no longer treated naturalistic depiction as the only serious artistic goal."}, {"id": "e01fa6b0-5c07-4220-8f09-b7df6041468d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Impressionism, Post-Impressionism, Symbolism, and early modernism", "children": [{"id": "bee05313-e7a3-4ec8-823d-b902529bb216", "type": "concept", "title": "Impressionist light, color, and urban modernity"}, {"id": "2d4547a3-0f04-4dda-adc5-9582acb41457", "type": "concept", "title": "Post-Impressionist structure, emotion, and symbolism"}, {"id": "8f2b13bc-c1b6-484e-bd1e-8d98795a2e46", "type": "concept", "title": "Early modern experiments in form, color, and subject"}], "description": "Students connect nineteenth-century experimentation to the later avant-garde rather than treating modernism as a sudden break."}]}, {"id": "37cc1aa9-c0b3-4695-a525-2cc21522d15f", "type": "topic", "title": "Cubism, Abstraction, and the Avant-Garde", "description": "This section develops the core modernist shift from illusionistic representation toward fragmented form, abstraction, experimentation, and artistic self-consciousness.", "children": [{"id": "de603223-8257-40ee-9bb9-0a7791348cf9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cubism and the reconstruction of seeing", "children": [{"id": "0a15dfc9-bce1-4a86-bf25-e79e6b5ebb36", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytic and Synthetic Cubism"}, {"id": "f820e768-d288-47bd-a9a3-68400223da28", "type": "concept", "title": "Collage, fragmentation, multiple viewpoints, and simultaneity"}, {"id": "f4baca56-27ed-4ee1-ba97-1514c2780770", "type": "concept", "title": "Still life, newspaper, typography, and modern materials"}], "description": "Cubism teaches students how artists dismantled single-point perspective and rebuilt pictorial space as a conceptual field."}, {"id": "ce9fe6df-a484-4902-986b-bb29b746085d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Abstraction and nonobjective art", "children": [{"id": "8b7f5722-543f-4f0f-97b8-8d5bd5dcded6", "type": "concept", "title": "Kandinsky, Malevich, Mondrian, and abstraction debates"}, {"id": "5d94b84f-3a6b-4ce3-81cd-52517157f9e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Color, geometry, rhythm, and composition"}, {"id": "f96cc6a9-71d1-4612-a184-fb9ac3c966cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Spiritual, utopian, and formalist arguments"}], "description": "Students learn the difference between abstraction from the visible world and nonobjective art built from color, line, form, and spiritual or formal ideas."}, {"id": "8a4b3320-cc6e-49df-8e30-e95cdbfa0753", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Avant-garde strategies", "children": [{"id": "58b016bc-cda3-40e0-a11b-55b9445c8e19", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifestos and artistic movements"}, {"id": "640db2f2-f51d-4faa-a447-d260c4012b2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Originality, shock, and anti-academic positions"}, {"id": "bc9749ae-d9b7-4b64-8a58-8878a9a37d25", "type": "concept", "title": "Medium specificity and the modernist canon"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces the avant-garde as a recurring modern strategy: rupture, manifesto, experiment, and provocation."}]}, {"id": "7e83695f-13b4-4e34-8a93-ab2d0385444f", "type": "topic", "title": "Dada, Surrealism, and the Critique of Reason", "description": "Students examine how artists responded to war, trauma, psychoanalysis, chance, and irrationality by attacking conventional art, language, and social order.", "children": [{"id": "8a8e970d-055f-4f0d-a031-5a2fcdf77a70", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dada and anti-art", "children": [{"id": "0c8a6726-7352-4974-b6f0-bb533a8dcf77", "type": "concept", "title": "Readymades, photomontage, performance, and nonsense"}, {"id": "964d9573-8dd5-4eb1-ad45-23de5be30662", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-bourgeois critique and anti-aesthetic tactics"}, {"id": "22d661cd-e60e-499d-83e8-5889fa79c379", "type": "concept", "title": "Zurich, Berlin, Paris, and New York Dada"}], "description": "Students examine Dada as a response to war and rationalized violence, using absurdity, chance, and negation as artistic tools."}, {"id": "d7f93410-a90e-4033-bf90-35900028f424", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Surrealism, psychoanalysis, and desire", "children": [{"id": "581494e8-f031-431b-8cba-5adf4c4e8e33", "type": "concept", "title": "Freud, dream logic, automatism, and chance"}, {"id": "c63bebdd-4d34-4b91-aa61-46f4ce6c5e4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Object, body, sexuality, and uncanny imagery"}, {"id": "8e1bc9b0-49d8-42d0-8f0d-621369561928", "type": "concept", "title": "Surrealism beyond Europe and later influence"}], "description": "Surrealism shows how dreams, the unconscious, automatism, and desire reshaped modern image-making."}, {"id": "714aacc6-cc19-42e1-a8ae-319fd9dfb694", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language, image, and political rupture", "children": [{"id": "7bcbe2bd-4c5e-487e-bc13-57cefed9ad37", "type": "concept", "title": "Text-image relations"}, {"id": "24e69a56-f9c6-4c43-b1f8-102c03638dd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Irrational juxtaposition and collage logic"}, {"id": "c3f6d58c-ac3c-4bf9-b57b-5aaa4a7a1f62", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolution, colonialism, gender, and power"}], "description": "Students learn to read art that disrupts conventional meaning rather than simply representing a topic."}]}, {"id": "e85a7303-75a5-4ca2-a234-538542a0891e", "type": "topic", "title": "Bauhaus, Constructivism, Design, and Modern Media", "description": "This topic links art to design, architecture, photography, typography, and political modernism, showing how modern art entered everyday life and mass communication.", "children": [{"id": "f3a747ec-5ac3-4215-b294-4658fe54e6a2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art, design, and everyday life", "children": [{"id": "c0ab9104-1899-400a-a8f1-b53572e4f749", "type": "concept", "title": "Bauhaus foundation course and workshop model"}, {"id": "53c8aeb9-5885-4a61-846b-796da043760d", "type": "concept", "title": "Form follows function, material truth, and design education"}, {"id": "0f6f476b-3705-4584-9278-52fe105c265d", "type": "concept", "title": "Typography, graphics, furniture, textiles, and architecture"}], "description": "This subtopic explains how modern art moved into furniture, posters, architecture, textiles, typography, and pedagogy."}, {"id": "aade541d-6a14-4ed1-a6ee-d5b326f1e948", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constructivism and political modernism", "children": [{"id": "384e1b24-31a9-4264-83eb-6acfc74afdef", "type": "concept", "title": "Russian Constructivism and Productivism"}, {"id": "2f48e024-7a92-45d7-91af-a064a3bd70d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Photomontage, posters, and political messaging"}, {"id": "da4bed28-0d4a-49ff-84d7-4823b295cb53", "type": "concept", "title": "Utopia, industry, labor, and collective life"}], "description": "Students connect abstraction and design to revolutionary politics, propaganda, industry, and public communication."}, {"id": "9c1e0a35-b219-4253-8506-16d4ce49125a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "New media of modernity", "children": [{"id": "8355a478-b535-43fe-ad6e-49591feb814f", "type": "concept", "title": "New Vision photography"}, {"id": "1249e13f-1d4a-4930-910a-2e05ac90930c", "type": "concept", "title": "Film, montage, and time-based perception"}, {"id": "ec91a6fd-54fd-4620-a221-fa369303c72f", "type": "concept", "title": "Reproducibility, circulation, and audience"}], "description": "Students see photography, film, print, and mass reproduction as central to modern art rather than secondary supports."}]}, {"id": "e6e9fc88-67aa-4283-a374-f0fd83e4dd50", "type": "topic", "title": "Postwar Abstraction and Abstract Expressionism", "description": "Students study postwar abstraction as a field of gesture, scale, material presence, ideology, and global cultural power.", "children": [{"id": "3114370c-87e9-4881-88f1-318ce6b67b57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gesture, scale, and material presence", "children": [{"id": "806bd7aa-f978-4b92-a810-d25ea90eca7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Action painting, color field, and all-over composition"}, {"id": "0d2228dc-d2a3-4e86-9fe5-21815e647c7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Canvas scale, bodily gesture, and mark"}, {"id": "66b5cfbf-75ff-4d40-9b85-05dbcf432deb", "type": "concept", "title": "Materiality, accident, and process"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze large-scale abstraction through process, embodiment, surface, and viewer encounter."}, {"id": "2dc16a78-63e3-465c-827a-f1f48007e53a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postwar politics and cultural power", "children": [{"id": "11e2cd6c-5275-429c-84d1-f3a1e72e8cec", "type": "concept", "title": "New York, Paris, exile, and postwar cultural centers"}, {"id": "b72a34fd-4463-4a2d-9b87-5ced9105eacb", "type": "concept", "title": "Cold War soft power and museum narratives"}, {"id": "0bf6f642-2aa3-4782-836e-2c3787ef3dcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Masculinity, heroism, and canon formation"}], "description": "This subtopic places abstraction inside Cold War politics, migration, trauma, and institutional promotion."}, {"id": "4b245726-6b48-4a52-b58c-89ad7aac7b24", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global and critical revisions", "children": [{"id": "3f32c566-5c79-4631-9226-bb4f8c578109", "type": "concept", "title": "Women abstractionists and overlooked networks"}, {"id": "b9358ffe-bcfa-4960-a940-f4c58800b1f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Black, Latin American, Asian, and European postwar abstraction"}, {"id": "faafa931-2aee-4293-86b1-413f080a2f42", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of formalism and nationalism"}], "description": "Students question inherited art-historical narratives and include artists marginalized by earlier survey frameworks."}]}, {"id": "05d62dfa-1375-42f0-b9b5-a21cac59c591", "type": "topic", "title": "Pop Art, Mass Culture, Minimalism, and Conceptual Art", "description": "This section tracks the move from expressive modernism to consumer culture, industrial form, language, systems, dematerialization, and institutional critique.", "children": [{"id": "d3820f56-c12a-4f77-b2be-50ff36e6ec8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pop Art and consumer culture", "children": [{"id": "fd894309-a784-465b-a631-6466c9d2c9ab", "type": "concept", "title": "British and American Pop"}, {"id": "b0ed1029-d6ea-4c2a-b917-b704fa27f14c", "type": "concept", "title": "Repetition, appropriation, irony, and branding"}, {"id": "e7e5eb1a-d105-4889-ba72-d158a1133168", "type": "concept", "title": "Celebrity, commodity, gender, and desire"}], "description": "Students analyze how artists used advertising, comics, television, celebrity, and commodity imagery to rethink modern life."}, {"id": "36740042-80a5-4bd3-8e6d-c8fd37c37634", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Minimalism and objecthood", "children": [{"id": "b461151b-d1d2-4f44-9abe-54c4c84a8367", "type": "concept", "title": "Specific objects, seriality, grids, and fabrication"}, {"id": "9622561c-06d6-4c84-af88-fa1e56b85636", "type": "concept", "title": "Phenomenology, site, and viewer movement"}, {"id": "a0433d32-75ae-47eb-ade2-be1da2bccf49", "type": "concept", "title": "Debates around theatricality and autonomy"}], "description": "Minimalism shifts attention to industrial materials, serial form, literal space, and the viewer's body."}, {"id": "5d0d506d-2e82-4e42-bac9-21230e7346e3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conceptual art and dematerialization", "children": [{"id": "17734961-6aae-408f-98c4-14b6c45be2f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Language, propositions, maps, and archives"}, {"id": "3ae9b4ce-11e2-43f2-989e-0f757c90c8cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Instruction pieces, systems, and data"}, {"id": "68562a3f-ea4f-477c-a2f4-6fa6b4db85ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, documentation, and institutional context"}], "description": "Students learn how ideas, language, systems, documentation, and instructions became artistic media."}]}, {"id": "35570d94-d1b9-436a-a6d4-e65f192faff8", "type": "topic", "title": "Performance, Video, Installation, Photography, and Expanded Media", "description": "Students learn how post-1960s artists expanded art beyond painting and sculpture into time, body, space, lens-based media, documentation, and participation.", "children": [{"id": "38631a5e-9a5e-48a0-8e51-f6650438bbcb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Performance and the body", "children": [{"id": "644727e2-fa2a-4498-b013-faeb2a6bc641", "type": "concept", "title": "Happenings, Fluxus, body art, and endurance"}, {"id": "98f09784-4ddf-4ec2-8544-3f0f17c36656", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, liveness, documentation, and reenactment"}, {"id": "2ab75934-7914-48f8-b8d8-2eb4090394f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent, vulnerability, risk, and ethics"}], "description": "Students study artworks that use action, duration, risk, presence, and documentation as primary material."}, {"id": "3ab85aa9-125c-4f5c-b0c6-43af01e5f374", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Video, photography, and lens-based art", "children": [{"id": "b956ebc6-d6fd-449f-b868-5357b7b6ca4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Early video art and television critique"}, {"id": "e66654cc-352a-4d07-b7cb-bdfd0b047d92", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography as concept, archive, staged image, and documentary"}, {"id": "26c77b37-c962-489e-b7b0-1b8f8e63b32d", "type": "concept", "title": "Time, sequence, projection, and screen culture"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces media that challenged traditional object-based art and transformed documentation into artwork."}, {"id": "e41ca962-36b7-4b1b-bb1f-642eadf5d87f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Installation and expanded space", "children": [{"id": "a1c39e04-30c7-4f46-8f4d-f79119d9ecb1", "type": "concept", "title": "Site specificity and environment"}, {"id": "bd7a9b24-ef20-4269-b5aa-f822b74cadcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Sound, light, projection, and multi-sensory form"}, {"id": "c0d49cdf-17fc-4001-8337-d84e8939c31d", "type": "concept", "title": "Viewer movement, participation, and documentation"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze art as spatial, immersive, site-responsive, and experiential."}]}, {"id": "c46f80d1-f78a-4230-ac81-b23e483f3a97", "type": "topic", "title": "Feminist, Queer, Black, Indigenous, and Decolonial Art Histories", "description": "This section centers artists and frameworks that challenged the exclusions of canonical modernism and remade the terms of art history.", "children": [{"id": "2c41cfe3-df93-4783-b81c-e07a5b8dc23e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist art and gender critique", "children": [{"id": "0918b34a-66c8-4c54-a1d3-dde760569e48", "type": "concept", "title": "Body politics, domestic labor, craft, and performance"}, {"id": "589e76f2-2e18-49ab-b63f-136cc3906b7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaborative practice and consciousness-raising"}, {"id": "928098ec-6c7f-48a3-8f3f-85a513b067c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Rewriting the canon and exhibition history"}], "description": "Students examine how feminist artists challenged representation, labor, authorship, institutions, and art-historical exclusion."}, {"id": "586e4d00-dc0f-4813-b023-8c69a0818b66", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer, Black, and Indigenous art histories", "children": [{"id": "91018a8c-cf01-42e3-bb08-0fec2083e600", "type": "concept", "title": "AIDS activism, queer archives, and chosen publics"}, {"id": "00caf497-5073-4e5e-b1f4-4c2eb02832a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Black aesthetics, abstraction, figuration, and institutional critique"}, {"id": "7b0af2e6-6a55-4b39-83b7-e76a83a3adf7", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous sovereignty, land, memory, and representation"}], "description": "This subtopic foregrounds artists whose work contests visibility, erasure, state violence, and dominant identity categories."}, {"id": "c8009a2d-ced7-4f01-a781-8d89af962c20", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decolonial methods", "children": [{"id": "de9ad9b1-ec36-4f01-a26f-aa2415e6f28b", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial archives and restitution debates"}, {"id": "a7136580-4542-4369-837e-c675034bf2ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora, hybridity, translation, and refusal"}, {"id": "3dfa95f6-f105-4ffb-a1c3-435cb8a59e18", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of citation, naming, and contextualization"}], "description": "Students learn to question colonial collecting, universalist narratives, and extractive museum histories."}]}, {"id": "1cdcad7e-97a6-4139-aa42-38a03da19e3a", "type": "topic", "title": "Identity-Based Art, Representation, Body, and Politics", "description": "Students analyze how artists use the body, portraiture, archives, material culture, and media critique to address identity, power, visibility, and social life.", "children": [{"id": "a60df92c-ca8f-4f99-b117-96d730da205e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and self-representation", "children": [{"id": "b6269f21-c16a-4926-8577-d6a851326f10", "type": "concept", "title": "Portraiture, self-fashioning, and visibility"}, {"id": "8956fc92-ea42-4ee5-9fc4-141924ea6ba6", "type": "concept", "title": "Appropriation, stereotype, and media critique"}, {"id": "8bbd9fb4-ac95-47e1-a014-3a5ec000315d", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, gaze, and reception"}], "description": "Students analyze who gets pictured, who controls images, and how artists rework stereotypes and visual power."}, {"id": "3c92535c-99ef-4e4d-a5fa-a58488f6fe25", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Body, material, and lived experience", "children": [{"id": "34596758-f641-4c85-b8c8-636de0521d0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment, disability, gender, race, and sexuality"}, {"id": "911264e5-6349-43c9-a385-4675857b6bca", "type": "concept", "title": "Hair, clothing, skin, food, ritual, and material culture"}, {"id": "a3edffe4-4cf0-4c2d-b25c-a5f34497a43f", "type": "concept", "title": "Trauma, care, intimacy, and vulnerability"}], "description": "This subtopic treats the body as image, site, archive, material, and political field."}, {"id": "8b51922b-09f4-4343-afe0-8e0d07ce0d57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Archives, memory, and counter-history", "children": [{"id": "993e223b-4a54-401b-badf-d586acf204a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Found photographs, family archives, and oral histories"}, {"id": "186090c0-74e8-49d9-9942-e878a08ff3fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memorials, and public memory"}, {"id": "e895127d-b7f9-4ad2-b81f-23f351a0a4cd", "type": "concept", "title": "Fiction, speculation, and counter-archives"}], "description": "Students learn how artists use archives to recover, dispute, invent, or complicate historical memory."}]}, {"id": "41a1ae97-0afa-4cf6-8cbe-7ce666f080f5", "type": "topic", "title": "Globalization, Biennials, Diaspora, and World Currents", "description": "This topic moves beyond a Euro-American sequence to examine global contemporary art circuits, migration, biennials, translation, and uneven cultural power.", "children": [{"id": "0c95242f-66e8-4fba-b9bb-cb4ed6a89196", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global contemporary art frameworks", "children": [{"id": "51a68100-0e89-4112-ab91-cd23013434bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Globalization, neoliberalism, and cultural circulation"}, {"id": "00676587-7bfe-4ba8-a17d-5a6963984e1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple modernisms and regional art histories"}, {"id": "fa28f38d-3008-41a0-a944-9b5549a0da4a", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation, context, and comparative method"}], "description": "Students move beyond a center-periphery model and learn to compare multiple modernities and contemporary conditions."}, {"id": "f846533e-81ca-4281-bc99-a9ce46eb42fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Biennials and international exhibitions", "children": [{"id": "6e9dbc44-12c8-4f6c-a46d-e54016a776e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Venice, Sao Paulo, Documenta, and newer biennials"}, {"id": "3761983d-9327-43dc-8da6-4aa5de74a2b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Curatorial themes, national pavilions, and geopolitics"}, {"id": "3892ca7f-67c4-45c2-b9ba-3262c2032f18", "type": "concept", "title": "Spectacle, tourism, funding, and critique"}], "description": "This subtopic explains how biennials and large exhibitions shape contemporary art visibility, discourse, and markets."}, {"id": "a654b911-801b-4474-9f05-227038b3a838", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diaspora, migration, and transnational practice", "children": [{"id": "638ae7eb-dbb8-439e-9b89-fa8b13013065", "type": "concept", "title": "Exile, migration, border regimes, and belonging"}, {"id": "b4022de0-414e-49ee-8914-5c72a242a0c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid materials, memory, and cultural translation"}, {"id": "d2492979-d58d-420f-9327-dbda5274020a", "type": "concept", "title": "Global networks and uneven access"}], "description": "Students study art shaped by movement across borders, languages, histories, and communities."}]}, {"id": "0ca73343-1206-4d51-85b8-27b66cd7e66e", "type": "topic", "title": "Institutional Critique, Museums, Markets, Galleries, and Alternative Spaces", "description": "Students investigate the systems that authorize, display, finance, collect, and narrate modern and contemporary art.", "children": [{"id": "5719bb54-9563-428a-bf0d-38bcedadf0ef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Institutional critique", "children": [{"id": "36546272-9206-4dba-8995-136b63ff7326", "type": "concept", "title": "Museum display, wall text, classification, and authority"}, {"id": "97eb2758-e600-4717-8068-9e64355cbcb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Artists who intervene in institutions"}, {"id": "22be068f-53bf-465e-9c82-a040ef8b3a1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, gender, labor, funding, and access"}], "description": "Students examine art that exposes the power structures of museums, collections, labels, archives, and display."}, {"id": "872f227e-3658-41a2-ad74-8fea9d696311", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Markets, galleries, and value", "children": [{"id": "b8ea8539-b22d-42cb-ba8b-972001042337", "type": "concept", "title": "Dealers, collectors, auctions, fairs, and speculation"}, {"id": "b42558cd-acac-41ae-a598-91638b6dce76", "type": "concept", "title": "Editioning, scarcity, provenance, and reputation"}, {"id": "220413a2-b7ce-40e7-ba2d-2b94a164cabc", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism, branding, and career infrastructure"}], "description": "This subtopic helps students understand how economic systems shape artistic visibility without reducing art to price."}, {"id": "ec164264-c7a7-4112-9916-b93125a538a9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alternative spaces and artist-run culture", "children": [{"id": "d1822ac9-2e9b-4972-a034-3385b5771fa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Artist-run spaces, collectives, mutual aid, and co-ops"}, {"id": "ed844299-01f7-4f3e-ada1-d51dda3cef28", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonprofits, community venues, online platforms, and pop-ups"}, {"id": "13b4e1a0-3f76-4273-9d8b-4ef5078d07e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Sustainability, governance, and public responsibility"}], "description": "Students learn why artists build spaces outside dominant institutions and how those spaces change art ecosystems."}]}, {"id": "0a4aedcb-6107-4e31-8dfe-18a47bce2ec1", "type": "topic", "title": "Public Art, Social Practice, Community Engagement, and Activism", "description": "This section studies art that operates in public, collaborative, educational, activist, and community-based contexts.", "children": [{"id": "c4d5aa4a-5b91-4a4e-8220-8d11778e4011", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public art and site", "children": [{"id": "f2c63cda-3107-48dc-8c5a-019e1c6cf73c", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memorials, murals, and site-responsive work"}, {"id": "024d575b-bcbb-407d-8042-f1822ecfd469", "type": "concept", "title": "Permits, stakeholders, conservation, and controversy"}, {"id": "1bceffe1-ffb0-4f6a-98aa-4f8a54eaabe7", "type": "concept", "title": "Public audience, accessibility, and interpretation"}], "description": "Students learn how public artworks negotiate place, audience, memory, infrastructure, and civic power."}, {"id": "eea6d8f4-54c9-4e42-80ba-3835413db1e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social practice and collaboration", "children": [{"id": "85bf2324-2d39-4efa-8271-2fc13a6628ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Collaboration, facilitation, workshops, and co-authorship"}, {"id": "84bfecd3-035c-4914-91ea-30830523ce75", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics of community engagement"}, {"id": "3843bb86-bb32-42aa-8f38-ad24bd8e7c5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation and evaluation of process-based work"}], "description": "This subtopic addresses participatory, community-based, educational, and process-centered art."}, {"id": "e9ad110d-0cb7-4647-bf0b-cbc29271234c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Art and activism", "children": [{"id": "403d8fc2-3465-4931-b2dd-00d15cf4d52d", "type": "concept", "title": "Posters, banners, performance, media campaigns, and occupations"}, {"id": "eae10d16-fce8-4753-a1d3-1b6fdfe7cfe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental, racial justice, labor, and housing activism"}, {"id": "d15e2841-8c08-4c5d-942e-df03d557041a", "type": "concept", "title": "Effectiveness, risk, care, and accountability"}], "description": "Students examine how art participates in protest, mutual aid, public discourse, and social change."}]}, {"id": "d78ea9f9-a557-4921-be99-4c370e3e4dd8", "type": "topic", "title": "Twenty-First-Century Contemporary Art: Digital Culture, Ecology, AI, and Networked Practice", "description": "Students examine current practices shaped by digital systems, climate crisis, surveillance, artificial intelligence, social media, and networked publics.", "children": [{"id": "91f1efad-e532-42b1-abf1-144e42b0fd23", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital and networked art", "children": [{"id": "fcf9b4c3-45a2-4683-920c-083f8e7399c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Internet art, post-internet art, and platform culture"}, {"id": "e54c028a-ba8a-4c9b-af3a-7c6eb7d530cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Memes, screens, avatars, games, and virtual spaces"}, {"id": "69aaabd3-7e13-4adb-83f8-00227a2756a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Data, surveillance, privacy, and algorithmic visibility"}], "description": "Students analyze art shaped by software, platforms, data, gaming, social media, and online circulation."}, {"id": "5b2b5e87-03d4-487e-924e-4b73cac6b0b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecology, climate, and material futures", "children": [{"id": "92cea01b-27ba-4e7c-b67d-8c80858e6a49", "type": "concept", "title": "Land, water, energy, waste, and petro-cultures"}, {"id": "e0c6125b-655a-4643-86ff-5470e9601050", "type": "concept", "title": "Eco-art, environmental justice, and Indigenous land relations"}, {"id": "f0812021-92ad-4867-9651-5740f73eb677", "type": "concept", "title": "Material lifecycle, conservation, and sustainability"}], "description": "This subtopic situates contemporary art within climate crisis, extractive economies, and ecological repair."}, {"id": "80e9d7d9-fdaa-4bd9-8725-792e710f9d20", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AI, automation, and authorship", "children": [{"id": "53c8363d-6802-4af7-b491-d73b42bbfe7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Generative image systems and prompt cultures"}, {"id": "2b8ee200-c313-46ad-844b-3c4ebd19fd1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, dataset ethics, bias, and intellectual property"}, {"id": "335a0636-48bb-47a9-b4d3-bfe557c8c76c", "type": "concept", "title": "Human-machine collaboration and critical response"}], "description": "Students consider how machine learning and automated image systems pressure older ideas of creativity, originality, and labor."}]}, {"id": "61f85d5d-c1e6-4028-a587-4d13c55e26b9", "type": "topic", "title": "Art Writing, Exhibition Analysis, Criticism, and Research Methods", "description": "This topic builds the practical scholarly skills needed to write clearly about artworks, exhibitions, artists, institutions, and debates.", "children": [{"id": "742f7f83-efab-4431-9bfc-c533dfa3dae3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual and formal analysis", "children": [{"id": "94d7e35d-b316-4970-b918-f137262a01d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition, color, scale, material, installation, and context"}, {"id": "14875b65-dc71-4004-be70-2f773dcab2df", "type": "concept", "title": "Description, analysis, interpretation, and argument"}, {"id": "3ebe950d-6731-45f2-bacf-19895ce7c3d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparison across works, media, and periods"}], "description": "Students practice describing what is present before jumping to interpretation, building precise evidence-based claims."}, {"id": "df8b1dfa-b887-49cf-b05b-e73be14299dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exhibition and criticism writing", "children": [{"id": "38586be0-3cbc-49aa-a923-bb4b3fd873ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibition review structure"}, {"id": "34090d52-0417-41df-a386-a7ce0e98b42d", "type": "concept", "title": "Artist statements, interviews, and criticism"}, {"id": "3dccb96b-d080-4ec7-93c4-4af64d14640a", "type": "concept", "title": "Tone, audience, evidence, and fair judgment"}], "description": "This subtopic turns looking into public-facing prose that can evaluate curatorial choices and viewer experience."}, {"id": "03282393-9c84-4a6a-9815-eac6b7bedb82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research methods and citation", "children": [{"id": "b8fe4d16-c2d2-42a9-9760-05d9bc7e4086", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated bibliography and source evaluation"}, {"id": "3dfa7624-b43d-44ce-8ba5-a602700a1cfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary documents, artist writings, reviews, and catalogues"}, {"id": "271ba232-ab5c-4b3c-999d-de71b632012e", "type": "concept", "title": "Image rights, captions, citation, and academic integrity"}], "description": "Students learn how to build credible art-historical arguments using images, scholarship, archives, and primary sources."}]}, {"id": "1591b031-046e-4336-8189-fd3fa83a44e0", "type": "topic", "title": "Curatorial and Research Capstone: Comparative Exhibition or Artist Case Study", "description": "The course culminates in a rigorous research project that asks students to synthesize movement history, theory, visual analysis, and contemporary stakes.", "children": [{"id": "b7a06ce6-824b-4b78-8c45-991d90a2e926", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question and scope", "children": [{"id": "d7a76682-13fc-48d4-bed6-d994c4227a96", "type": "concept", "title": "Movement, artist, institution, medium, or exhibition focus"}, {"id": "39634e4c-d241-4f7a-8a4f-1c411786669a", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative frame and stakes"}, {"id": "fe05a09b-04b9-409a-8de1-a304ad8c7ea1", "type": "concept", "title": "Feasible scope, timeline, and evidence plan"}], "description": "Students turn a broad interest into a focused, answerable art-historical or curatorial problem."}, {"id": "ef886b33-cd30-43da-9db1-b9683776b94e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case study development", "children": [{"id": "15ca9644-9342-4c65-9337-d201cfa21069", "type": "concept", "title": "Object selection and image dossier"}, {"id": "0919a7d9-c347-483f-9c1e-63660656f0e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Secondary scholarship and primary documents"}, {"id": "9666e356-d578-4c39-a997-e29498ded7df", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument map and revision through feedback"}], "description": "This subtopic teaches students to connect close looking with history, theory, and context."}, {"id": "63c49c23-502d-4620-85cf-7d78d35b1085", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final presentation and synthesis", "children": [{"id": "19e39dc9-09d7-4c01-905e-804eccab90f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Research paper or curatorial proposal"}, {"id": "cc711446-9434-4a42-b001-d72682f61952", "type": "concept", "title": "Slide presentation, wall text, or exhibition checklist"}, {"id": "78a54d9d-c273-468e-bdbe-46616bf49453", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection on contemporary relevance and limitations"}], "description": "Students demonstrate mastery through a polished written and visual presentation that communicates to an informed audience."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$mocajson$::jsonb,
  true,
